{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-14050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/0470661491c0d9d44ecbbc3c87af70d4ff334f1ffd2ca56b68bd4c126f3d88de.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-\\frac{7}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-\\frac{7}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{7}{4}+4) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+4) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 4+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $7$ and $-1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14050" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c37a0515d7324b74faed7cf8635d16347c480ba7552e6417cc3be74c1f43f33"} {"question" "q-14050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$7$ and $-1$"] ["text" "$7$ and $-2$"] ["text" "$8$ and $-1$"] ["text" "$8$ and $-3$"] ["text" "$9$ and $-2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14050" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$7$ and $-1$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/f2de46decf74a41e6375325e6eeb015113311b00300eb639d0f1f85a9f779337.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-5,-6)$ and passes through the point $(-1,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-5,-6)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =4p(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-1,2),$ we substitute $x=-1$ and $y=2$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1+5)^{2} & =4p(2+6) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(y+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)^{2} & =2(0+6) \\\\ (x+5)^{2} & =12 \\\\ x+5 & =\\pm \\sqrt{12} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts are $\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14052" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c37a0515d7324b74faed7cf8635d16347c480ba7552e6417cc3be74c1f43f33"} {"question" "q-14052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}+3"] ["math" "\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5"] ["math" "\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-6"] ["math" "\\pm 3\\sqrt{3}-5"] ["math" "\\pm \\sqrt{3}-5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14052" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}-5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/9b7acbd4f24b6bbd20dd00fc042405324c069d28db0102c2cd7dd49e6b3bbccd.png)\n\nWhat are the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,4)$ and passes through the point $(-6,8).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}=4p(x-2).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,8),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=8$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8-4)^{2} & =4p(-6-2) \\\\ 16 & =4p(-8) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =-2(0-2) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2+4 \\\\ y & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $2$ and  $6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14241" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c37a0515d7324b74faed7cf8635d16347c480ba7552e6417cc3be74c1f43f33"} {"question" "q-14241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$1$ and $6$"] ["text" "$1$ and $7$"] ["text" "$2$ and $6$"] ["text" "$2$ and $7$"] ["text" "$3$ and $5$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-14241" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$2$ and $6$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/8b48ef824df51aa680a727dc46d04e0936f61729475026b90378e153db503c76.png)\n\nThe value of the $x$-intercept of the parabola shown above is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-6,2)$ and passes through the point $(-7,4).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-6,2)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y-2)^{2}=4p(x+6).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-7,4),$ we substitute $x=-7$ and $y=4$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-2)^{2} & =4p(-7+6) \\\\ (2)^{2} & =4p\\cdot (-1) \\\\ 4 & =-4p \\\\ p & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =-4(x+6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+2)^{2} & =-4(x+6) \\\\ 4 & =-4(x+6) \\\\ -\\frac{4}{4} & =x+6 \\\\ x & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $-7.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c37a0515d7324b74faed7cf8635d16347c480ba7552e6417cc3be74c1f43f33"} {"question" "q-251692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/assets/b03beb34efa3fef7aaf18a879eb628748d86dc1b47eb4b45f60c4d02935e372b.png)\n\nThe value of the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above  is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(4,-3)$ and passes through the point $(10,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(4,-3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}=4p(y+3).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(10,6),$ we substitute $x=10$ and $y=6$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(10-4)^{2} & =4p(6+3) \\\\ 36 & =4p(9) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4)^{2} & =4(y+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-4)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ 16 & =4(y+3) \\\\ 4 & =y+3 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251800" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5c37a0515d7324b74faed7cf8635d16347c480ba7552e6417cc3be74c1f43f33"} {"question" "q-251800" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"} {"question" "q-251800" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14005477/state.json" "file_sha256" "92ab4a314f2edb831c36ca6568c11ec8203b13281c4acf0d675c8951e11f8070"}] "usage" {"q-14052" []} "basis" 1108 "retractions" [[17592186097028 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1132/Source/Images/q-14052.png)\n\nWhat are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186094575 17592186094576 17592186094577 17592186094578 17592186094579] "immutable_fields" [[17592186094574 :answer-field/correct] [17592186094574 :answer-f